docs/CLI.md:1115:The Claude Code side still uses the hooks, settings, and skills under `.claude/`. Codex does not consume these Claude runtime configs and does not read `.lattice-codex/settings.json`. What Proofbound generates for Codex is adapter assets: full mode generates the agent prompts from `.claude/agents` into `.lattice-codex/agents` in a fully controlled way; thin mode instead records the package prompt registry's version and material hash in `.lattice-codex/lattice.json`, without requiring local prompt copies. The Codex native PreToolUse hook uses the official lowercase `.codex/hooks.json` and `.codex/hooks/lattice-scope-pretool.cjs`; on a case-insensitive file system it may appear as the `.lattice-codex/hooks` alias. `.lattice-codex/lattice.json` also records the Proofbound-managed adapter's recommended entry points, the `source=codex` activity tag, the session environment variables, and the explicitly excluded runtime assets. The Codex-side notes live in:
docs/CLI.md:1119:- `.lattice/lattice.json` (global-thin default init primary; full/thin mode projects may use `.lattice-codex/lattice.json` instead)
docs/CLI.md:1120:- [.lattice-codex/agents/](/Users/jek/Documents/Work/Orchestrate/.lattice-codex/agents:1) (full mode materialization; can be omitted in thin mode)
docs/CLI.md:1122:In a full-mode repository, `.lattice-codex/agents` is fully generated from `.claude/agents` through a controlled transform and protected against later drift by `npm run test:codex-assets`; in a thin-mode repository, `node bin/lattice-codex.js verify_codex` validates the registry manifest/hash/renderability.
